      excerpt: "A web developer in Cagayan de Oro: should you hire a local office or a remote studio? Here is what to check on process, price and ownership.",
      content: `
        <p>A web developer in Cagayan de Oro gives you two choices: a local office you can visit in person, or a remote studio that works entirely online. For most CDO businesses, a structured remote studio is the more practical option — the build runs on a written brief, a staging link, and a short review call rather than a series of in-person meetings, and the price difference between local and remote can be significant.</p>
        <h2>What a local developer in Cagayan de Oro offers</h2>
        <p>The case for local is straightforward: you can sit down with someone face to face, walk through your brief, and call them directly if something needs fixing after launch. A local developer who knows CDO will understand the kinds of businesses common to the city and the expectations of your customers.</p>
        <p>The limitation is usually capacity and process. An individual freelancer juggling several clients may have no fixed handover process — no written brief, no staging link, no milestone schedule. That flexibility can work in your favour if you need constant back-and-forth, but it can also mean a project that drifts in scope, takes longer than expected, or ends without a clean handover of your files.</p>
        <h2>What a remote studio brings to a CDO project</h2>
        <p>A remote web studio — whether based in Iligan City, Cebu, Manila, or anywhere else in the Philippines — works from a document brief, a project thread, and a staging link you review in your own time. You do not need to be available for a meeting every time a design decision needs to be made.</p>
        <p>For Northern Mindanao businesses, a Philippine studio is rarely more than a video call away. Most studios work 8:30am to 5:00pm on weekdays, which means a message in the morning typically gets a same-day reply. A seven-day fixed-price build means you know the cost upfront, the site is yours outright on delivery, and there is no lock-in contract. If you prefer face-to-face meetings, Fazed Digital in Iligan City is available in person only for clients in or near Iligan — everyone in CDO and beyond works by video call.</p>
        <h2>What to check before you decide</h2>
        <p>Whether you go local or remote, ask the same questions of anyone you are considering.</p>
        <p>First, look at their portfolio on a phone, not a desktop. Does each site load quickly? Is the layout readable on a small screen? Does every page have a clear way to contact the business? A slow or cluttered portfolio is a warning regardless of where the studio is located.</p>
        <p>Second, ask how the project runs. When does work start? When do you see the first draft? How many rounds of revisions are included, and what happens if you want a change outside that scope? A studio that answers these questions clearly and puts them in writing is far more likely to deliver on time.</p>
        <p>Third, confirm ownership. You should own the domain, the site files, and all content outright once you have paid in full. This is standard with reputable studios. If a studio is vague about who keeps what after the project ends, resolve that before work starts, not after.</p>
        <p>For a fuller guide to comparing your options, the post on <a href="https://www.fazeddigital.com/blog/how-to-choose-web-design-agency-philippines">how to choose a web design agency in the Philippines</a> covers red flags, portfolio checks, and what a proper brief should include.</p>
        <h2>What web development costs in the Philippines in 2026</h2>
        <p>Web development prices in the Philippines vary widely. A five-page small business site typically ranges from ₱15,000 to ₱60,000 for standard builds, based on typical ranges in agency price guides published by directories such as GoodFirms — individual quotes depend on your brief and who you hire.</p>
        <p>Our packages are ₱19,999 (Starter, 5 pages), ₱34,999 (Growth, 8 pages), and ₱59,999 (Pro, 10+ pages), all launched in seven business days when your content arrives on Day 1 and feedback comes within 24 hours at each review stage. If we miss the deadline on our side, you get 20% off the project fee. Payment by GCash, Maya, BPI or Metrobank bank transfer, or Wise. Standard terms are a 50% deposit before work starts; IP passes to you on full payment.</p>
        <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
        <p><strong>Do I need to meet my web developer in person?</strong> Not for most projects. A brief by email or Google Meet, feedback on a staging link, and a final walkthrough call before launch is a practical and complete workflow. In-person meetings are useful if your developer is nearby, but they are rarely essential for a standard small business website.</p>
        <p><strong>How long does a website take to build?</strong> A structured studio can build a five-to-ten-page business site in seven business days, provided your content — copy, logo, and images — arrives on Day 1. Without that content ready, the build waits. Gather your materials before you sign anything and the timeline is reliable.</p>
        <p><strong>Can I update my website myself after launch?</strong> It depends on how the site is built. Ask the studio before you commit whether a CMS is included and what it costs to make changes later if it is not. Any honest studio will answer this plainly, and the answer matters more than most owners realise until they need their first update.</p>
        <p>To see our packages and past work serving Northern Mindanao, visit our <a href="https://www.fazeddigital.com/web-design-iligan-city">web design Iligan City and Northern Mindanao page</a>.</p>
      `,
