        <p>Manila is 12–13 hours ahead of US Eastern time (15–16 hours ahead of Pacific), so most of the work runs asynchronously. A well-run studio sends you a staging link for review each evening US time, you review it the next morning, and your feedback arrives at the start of their working day. A tight, focused build often moves faster this way than a project requiring constant back-and-forth calls.</p>
        <p><strong>The process works best with a clear brief upfront.</strong> Before the project starts, you supply your logo, images, brand colors, page copy, and a couple of reference sites. The studio builds against the brief and sends you a staging link at each milestone. Most studios schedule a kickoff call and a final walkthrough during the overlap window: early morning US Eastern time, late afternoon Manila time.</p>
        <h2>What you pay and how payments work</h2>
        <p>Fixed-price packages differ from studio to studio. Ours are $499 (Starter), $899 (Growth) and $1,499 (Pro), each delivered in 7 business days; see <a href="https://www.fazeddigital.com/lp/7-day-website">our 7-day website packages</a>. Our custom builds (web apps, portals, ecommerce platforms) are quoted after a discovery call and typically fall between $5,000 and $20,000, depending on scope. Whoever you hire, a reputable studio gives you a fixed quote before you commit.</p>
        <p>Most Philippine studios accept payment by Wise or international bank transfer. Wise is the most common choice for US clients — transfers are faster and cheaper than a traditional wire. Standard terms are a 50% deposit before work starts, with the balance due on delivery. You own the domain, site files, and all content once payment is complete, with no lock-in contract.</p>
        <h2>How to vet a studio before you hire</h2>
        <p>Start with the portfolio. A credible studio shows you completed projects on a live domain — not just mockups or screenshots. If the work matches the style and complexity you need, check how long the studio has been operating. A team running for three or more years has had the time to prove itself through repeat clients and referrals.</p>
        <p><strong>Ask three questions in your first email:</strong> What happens if the site is not delivered on time? Who owns the domain and files after payment? What if I need changes six months from now? Clear, written answers signal a professional operation. A studio that deflects on ownership or timelines is a risk worth avoiding. For a deeper checklist, our guide on <a href="https://www.fazeddigital.com/blog/how-to-choose-web-design-agency-philippines">choosing a web design agency in the Philippines</a> covers pricing norms, red flags, and what a solid contract should include.</p>
        <p>One reference point from our own work: A Framing Company, a framing contractor in Utah, needed a website that showed their finished builds to contractors and developers. We designed and built a portfolio-led WordPress site in 6 weeks, and it is now a core part of how they pitch new work. Read the <a href="https://www.fazeddigital.com/work/01">A Framing Company case study</a>. It is an example of the process, not a promise of your outcome.</p>
