      slug: 'rebranding-when-and-how',
      title: "When to Rebrand (And When You Really Shouldn't)",
      category: 'Branding',
      date: '25 Sep 2026',
      readTime: '5 min read',
      author: 'Lord Arcamo',
      authorRole: 'Founder',
      authorPhoto: '/images/team-lord.jpeg',
      image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1434626881859-194d67b2b86f?w=1200&h=630&fit=crop&auto=format&q=80',
      excerpt: "Rebranding is expensive, disruptive, and often unnecessary. Here's how to honestly assess whether your brand needs a refresh — or whether the real problem is something else entirely.",
      content: `
        <p>Every few months a business owner comes to us and says, "We need a rebrand." Sometimes they're right. More often, what they actually need is better marketing execution, a clearer value proposition, or a new website — not a new logo. Rebranding for the wrong reasons wastes money and resets brand equity you've spent years building.</p>
        <h2>Legitimate reasons to rebrand</h2>
        <p><strong>Your business has fundamentally changed.</strong> You've pivoted to a different market, expanded your service offering significantly, or merged with another company. The brand no longer describes what you actually do or who you serve. This is the clearest signal that a rebrand is warranted.</p>
        <p><strong>Your audience has changed.</strong> You started targeting small local clients and now you're pitching enterprise contracts. Your brand aesthetic and voice need to match where you're going, not where you've been.</p>
        <p><strong>Your brand is genuinely creating friction.</strong> Prospects regularly misunderstand your positioning. Your name is being confused with a competitor. You're in a new market where your brand carries no meaning. These are real, measurable problems that a rebrand can solve.</p>
        <h2>Bad reasons to rebrand</h2>
        <p><strong>You're bored with it.</strong> You've looked at your logo 10,000 times and you're tired of it. Your customers haven't. Brand fatigue is an internal problem — don't solve it at the expense of the recognition you've built externally.</p>
        <p><strong>Sales are down.</strong> A rebrand is not a sales strategy. If revenue is declining, the causes are almost always in your offer, pricing, sales process, or marketing channels — not your logo. Fix the real problem first.</p>
        <p><strong>A competitor rebranded.</strong> Their reasons and yours are different. Don't let a competitor's decision push you into an expensive exercise that doesn't serve your business.</p>
        <h2>The middle path: a brand refresh</h2>
        <p>Most businesses don't need a full rebrand — they need a refresh. Updating the typography, tightening the colour palette, modernising the logo mark without abandoning it, and clarifying the brand voice. This preserves the equity you have while bringing the brand up to date. It's faster, cheaper, and usually more strategically sound than starting from zero.</p>
        <h2>Before you decide, test the hypothesis</h2>
        <p>Ask ten customers — not colleagues, not family — what words come to mind when they think of your brand. Ask what they think you do. Ask if they'd recommend you. Their answers will tell you whether a brand problem actually exists. If it doesn't, spend your rebrand budget on marketing instead.</p>
      `,
    },
    {
      slug: 'landing-page-conversion-design',
      title: "The Anatomy of a High-Converting Landing Page",
      category: 'Design',
      date: '18 Sep 2026',
      readTime: '7 min read',
      author: 'Lord Arcamo',
      authorRole: 'Founder',
      authorPhoto: '/images/team-lord.jpeg',
      image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1581291518857-4d27a540d9f2?w=1200&h=630&fit=crop&auto=format&q=80',
      excerpt: "Most landing pages fail at the same predictable points. Here's the structure that works — backed by what we've seen across dozens of client campaigns.",
      content: `
        <p>A landing page has one job: convert a specific visitor to a specific action. Not to explain everything you do. Not to showcase your full portfolio. One page, one goal, one conversion. The pages that fail almost always do so because they try to do too much.</p>
        <h2>Above the fold: the only section that matters first</h2>
        <p>Before your visitor scrolls — often within 3 seconds — they've made a judgment. The above-the-fold section must answer three questions immediately: What is this? Who is it for? Why should I care? Your headline answers what and for whom. Your subheadline answers why. Your primary CTA button answers what to do next.</p>
        <p>The most common mistake here is leading with your company name and tagline. Nobody cares yet. Lead with the outcome the visitor gets, not who you are. "Get a website that converts visitors into customers" outperforms "Welcome to Fazed Digital" every single time.</p>
        <h2>Social proof placed before the ask</h2>
        <p>Trust must come before commitment. A visitor who doesn't trust you won't convert, no matter how compelling your offer. Place testimonials, client logos, case study callouts, or review ratings early — ideally in the first scroll, before your primary CTA. Specific social proof outperforms generic: "We increased conversions by 43% in 60 days" beats "Great to work with!" by a significant margin.</p>
        <h2>The offer section: clarity over creativity</h2>
        <p>Explain what the visitor gets, in plain language, in three to five bullet points. Use the visitor's language, not yours — describe outcomes, not features. "A website live in 4 weeks" is clearer than "Rapid deployment methodology." Avoid jargon. Every word that requires explanation is a word that loses people.</p>
        <h2>Objection handling built into the page</h2>
        <p>Every visitor has silent objections: Is this too expensive? Will it actually work for my industry? What if I'm not happy? Address these directly in an FAQ section or woven into your copy. Naming the objection and answering it honestly converts better than pretending it doesn't exist. Transparency builds the trust that gets people to click.</p>
        <h2>One CTA, repeated strategically</h2>
        <p>Your call to action should appear three times on most landing pages: above the fold, after the proof section, and at the bottom. It should say the same thing every time — not "Learn More" in one place and "Get Started" in another. Consistency reduces decision fatigue. Make the button text describe the action specifically: "Book a Free Strategy Call" is stronger than "Submit."</p>
        <h2>Speed and mobile — the invisible conversion killers</h2>
        <p>A landing page that takes more than 3 seconds to load on a mobile device loses a significant portion of its visitors before they've read a word. Mobile now accounts for over 60% of web traffic. If your landing page isn't designed and tested on mobile first, you're optimising for the minority. Run every page through Google's PageSpeed Insights before you run any paid traffic to it.</p>
      `,
    },
    {
      slug: 'wordpress-vs-custom-build',
      title: "WordPress vs Custom Build: Which Is Right for Your Business?",
      category: 'Development',
      date: '10 Sep 2026',
      readTime: '6 min read',
      author: 'Justin Sumaya',
      authorRole: 'Head of Development',
      authorPhoto: 'https://fazeddigital.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/151652330_3908937289163068_8338977159966182887_n-450x450.jpg',
      image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1461749280684-dccba630e2f6?w=1200&h=630&fit=crop&auto=format&q=80',
      excerpt: "It's not a technical decision — it's a business one. Here's the honest breakdown of when WordPress wins and when a custom build is worth the investment.",
      content: `
        <p>We build on both WordPress and custom stacks — Astro, Next.js, and fully bespoke solutions — and we have no agenda either way. What we do have is a clear view of which approach serves which kind of business. Here's the honest breakdown.</p>
        <h2>The case for WordPress</h2>
        <p>WordPress powers around 43% of all websites for a reason: it's mature, well-supported, and editable by anyone. For businesses that need to update their own content regularly — adding blog posts, changing service descriptions, updating team pages — WordPress's admin interface is genuinely easy to use without technical knowledge.</p>
        <p>The plugin ecosystem is massive. Need a booking system, a payment gateway, a contact form with conditional logic, or a membership area? There's a plugin for it, and it typically costs a fraction of what a custom-built equivalent would. For small businesses with standard requirements, WordPress is almost always the faster, more affordable path.</p>
        <h2>Where WordPress falls short</h2>
        <p>Performance is WordPress's biggest liability. A poorly optimised WordPress site — and most are, straight out of the box — scores poorly on Core Web Vitals, loads slowly on mobile, and requires ongoing maintenance to stay secure. Plugins conflict. Updates break things. Hosting matters enormously and most budget hosts are inadequate for anything beyond a basic brochure site.</p>
        <p>WordPress also creates dependency on its own ecosystem. When you need something it doesn't do well — complex data relationships, real-time features, highly customised user experiences — you're fighting the platform rather than building with it.</p>
        <h2>The case for a custom build</h2>
        <p>A custom build starts from nothing and builds exactly what you need — no more. This means cleaner code, better performance, no unnecessary plugin weight, and complete design freedom. For businesses where the website is a core product (SaaS platforms, marketplaces, booking systems with complex logic), custom is not a luxury — it's a requirement.</p>
        <p>Static site generators like Astro produce near-perfect performance scores by default. For marketing sites and content-heavy platforms where speed directly impacts conversions and SEO, this matters enormously. A custom Astro build consistently outperforms a WordPress site on every Core Web Vitals metric.</p>
        <h2>The honest recommendation</h2>
        <p>For most small business brochure sites, service businesses, and content blogs: WordPress, built well, with quality hosting. For e-commerce beyond basic WooCommerce: evaluate purpose-built platforms like Shopify first. For marketing sites where performance and SEO are priorities, or for any business where the website IS the product: custom build. When in doubt, ask what you'll need to change in 12 months. The answer usually makes the decision obvious.</p>
