      slug: 'keyword-research-small-business',
      title: "Keyword Research for Small Businesses: Find the Terms That Actually Win",
      category: 'SEO',
      date: '01 Sep 2026',
      readTime: '5 min read',
      author: 'Russel Heyrana',
      authorRole: 'Marketing Director',
      authorPhoto: '/images/team-russel.jpeg',
      image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1460925895917-afdab827c52f?w=1200&h=630&fit=crop&auto=format&q=80',
      excerpt: "Ranking for a keyword nobody searches is pointless. Ranking for one you can't compete on is worse. Here's how to find the terms your business can realistically own.",
      content: `
        <p>The most common keyword research mistake small businesses make is going after the obvious terms — "web design," "accounting services," "real estate" — and then wondering why they're stuck on page 8. Those keywords are dominated by companies with enormous budgets and years of domain authority. You don't win by competing there. You win by finding the terms your ideal customers are actually searching, that your competitors haven't fully addressed.</p>
        <h2>Start with your customer's language, not yours</h2>
        <p>Your customers don't search the way you talk about your business. An accountant's client doesn't search "bookkeeping services for SMEs" — they search "how to do BIR filing for small business" or "accountant for online sellers Philippines." The gap between industry language and customer language is where keyword opportunities live.</p>
        <p>The fastest way to close this gap: talk to five actual customers and ask what they searched for before they found you. Their exact phrases are your starting point. Google's autocomplete and "People also ask" boxes add more. These free tools surface real search behavior without any software required.</p>
        <h2>The keyword matrix: volume vs. competition vs. intent</h2>
        <p>Every keyword sits on three dimensions. <strong>Volume</strong>: how many people search it per month. <strong>Competition</strong>: how hard it is to rank for it (keyword difficulty score). <strong>Intent</strong>: what the searcher is trying to do — research, compare, or buy.</p>
        <p>The sweet spot for small businesses is low-to-medium competition, clear buying intent, and enough volume to matter. A keyword with 200 monthly searches and low competition, searched by people ready to hire, is worth far more than a keyword with 10,000 monthly searches dominated by major brands.</p>
        <h2>Free tools that are good enough to start</h2>
        <p>Google Search Console shows you what you already rank for — start there. Google's Keyword Planner (free with a Google Ads account) gives volume ranges. Ubersuggest's free tier provides difficulty scores and related terms. Ahrefs and SEMrush are more powerful but unnecessary until you've exhausted the free options. Most small businesses can build a strong keyword strategy without spending a peso on tools.</p>
        <h2>Long-tail keywords: where small businesses actually win</h2>
        <p>Long-tail keywords are longer, more specific phrases — "affordable web design for restaurants in Cebu" rather than "web design Philippines." They have lower volume individually, but collectively they account for the majority of all searches. They're also dramatically easier to rank for, and the people searching them are usually further along in the buying process. Building content around 20 well-chosen long-tail keywords will almost always outperform chasing 3 competitive head terms.</p>
        <h2>Turning keywords into content</h2>
        <p>Each keyword cluster should map to one piece of content — a service page, a blog post, or a location page. The page should answer the searcher's question completely, use the keyword naturally in the title, H1, and first paragraph, and demonstrate genuine expertise on the topic. Thin content that merely mentions a keyword ranks nowhere. Thorough content that genuinely helps the searcher is what earns and holds rankings over time.</p>
      `,
