    {
      slug: 'website-speed-fixes',
      title: "Why Is My Website So Slow? 7 Common Causes and How to Fix Them",
      category: 'Development',
      date: '16 Jul 2026',
      readTime: '3 min read',
      author: 'Justin Sumaya',
      authorRole: 'Head of Development',
      authorPhoto: 'https://fazeddigital.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/151652330_3908937289163068_8338977159966182887_n-450x450.jpg',
      image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1498050108023-c5249f4df085?w=1200&h=630&fit=crop&auto=format&q=80',
      excerpt: "A slow website loses visitors, rankings and sales. Here are the seven most common reasons small business sites load slowly, and the practical fix for each one.",
      content: `
        <p>Speed is one of the few website problems that hurts you three ways at once. Visitors leave before the page loads, Google uses page experience as a ranking factor, and slow checkout or booking flows lose sales directly. On mobile data, which is how most Filipinos browse, every extra second matters even more. Here are the seven causes we see most often when auditing slow small business websites.</p>
        <h2>1. Oversized images</h2>
        <p>This is the number one culprit. A photo straight from a phone or camera can be 4–8MB. Upload ten of those on a homepage and you have a page heavier than many mobile games. <strong>The fix:</strong> resize images to the largest size they will actually display, compress them, and serve modern formats like WebP or AVIF. Lazy-load images below the fold so they only download when needed. This alone often cuts page weight by more than half.</p>
        <h2>2. Too many plugins</h2>
        <p>On WordPress especially, every plugin can add scripts, styles and database queries to every page, even pages that do not use it. Sites with 30 or more plugins are common, and many of those plugins are inactive or duplicated. <strong>The fix:</strong> audit your plugins, remove anything unused, and replace heavy multi-purpose plugins with lighter, single-purpose alternatives or simple custom code.</p>
        <h2>3. Cheap or overcrowded hosting</h2>
        <p>Very cheap shared hosting puts hundreds of sites on one server. When a neighbour gets a traffic spike, your site slows down. Servers located far from your customers also add delay. <strong>The fix:</strong> choose hosting with good performance reviews and servers in or near your market, or move to a modern static hosting platform with a global CDN.</p>
        <h2>4. Heavy page builders and themes</h2>
        <p>Drag-and-drop builders and do-everything themes are convenient, but they often load large amounts of code to support features you never use. <strong>The fix:</strong> if speed is critical, consider a leaner theme or a modern framework build. Sites built with tools like Astro ship little or no JavaScript by default, which is why they regularly score in the high 90s on PageSpeed.</p>
        <h2>5. Third-party scripts</h2>
        <p>Chat widgets, social feeds, multiple analytics tools, ad pixels and embedded videos each load code from other servers you do not control. Individually small, together they can add seconds. <strong>The fix:</strong> list every third-party script, remove what you do not actively use, and load the rest after the main content, or only when a visitor interacts.</p>
        <h2>6. No caching</h2>
        <p>Without caching, your server rebuilds every page from scratch for every visitor. <strong>The fix:</strong> enable page caching on your server or via a plugin, set proper browser caching headers so returning visitors do not re-download unchanged files, and put a CDN in front of the site.</p>
        <h2>7. Render-blocking fonts and CSS</h2>
        <p>Custom fonts and large stylesheets loaded in the wrong way force the browser to wait before showing anything. <strong>The fix:</strong> load fonts asynchronously, limit the number of font weights, and inline the small amount of CSS needed for the first screen.</p>
        <h2>How to measure your progress</h2>
        <p>Run your homepage and key service pages through Google PageSpeed Insights, focusing on mobile scores and the Core Web Vitals: Largest Contentful Paint under 2.5 seconds is the target to aim for. Fix the biggest opportunities first and re-test after each change.</p>
        <p>Sometimes the fastest fix is a rebuild. Our <a href="/lp/7-day-website">7-Day Website Package</a> delivers lightweight, performance-first sites designed to load in under two seconds on mobile.</p>
      `,
    },
