      content: `
        <p>In the Philippines, Facebook is where business happens. Customers discover shops through their feed, ask questions over Messenger and place orders in the comments. It is no surprise that many small businesses ask us: "Do I even need a website if my Facebook page is doing well?" The honest answer is yes, and not because a website replaces Facebook, but because relying on Facebook alone is a risk most owners do not see until it hurts.</p>
        <h2>You do not own your Facebook page</h2>
        <p>Your page, your followers and your content live on a platform whose rules can change overnight. Pages get restricted for reasons that are hard to appeal. Accounts get hacked. Organic reach has declined steadily for years, so posts reach a small fraction of your followers unless you pay to boost them. <strong>A website is the one online asset you fully control.</strong> Nobody can switch it off, reduce its reach or change the rules on you.</p>
        <h2>Google does not show Facebook posts for most searches</h2>
        <p>When someone searches "catering services in Cagayan de Oro" or "affordable dentist near me", Google mostly shows websites and Google Business Profiles, not Facebook posts. Customers who search, rather than scroll, are often the most ready to buy. Without a website, you are invisible at the exact moment of highest intent.</p>
        <h2>Websites build trust that Facebook cannot</h2>
        <p>Corporate clients, overseas customers and larger buyers often check for a proper website before contacting a business. A professional site with your own domain and email address, like hello@yourbusiness.com instead of a Gmail account, signals that you are established and serious. For B2B services especially, no website can quietly cost you deals you never knew about.</p>
        <h2>What Facebook does better</h2>
        <p>To be fair, Facebook is excellent for things websites are not: reaching people who were not searching for you, building community, sharing quick updates and handling fast conversations over Messenger. The goal is not to choose one. It is to give each a clear job.</p>
        <h2>How to make them work together</h2>
        <p>Use <strong>Facebook for discovery and conversation</strong>, and your <strong>website as the home base</strong> where customers get the full picture and take action. In practice: put your website link in your page bio, post buttons and ad destinations. Use your website for things Facebook handles poorly, such as detailed service pages, pricing, portfolios, booking forms, FAQs and blog content that ranks on Google. Add a Messenger chat button to your website so visitors can still message you the way they prefer.</p>
        <p>Install the Meta Pixel on your website so you can retarget visitors with Facebook ads. Someone who browsed your pricing page but did not enquire is a far warmer audience than a cold one.</p>
        <h2>What a small business website really needs</h2>
        <p>You do not need a huge site. A clear homepage, a page per main service, an about page with real photos, a contact page with a form and map, and a way to showcase reviews will put you ahead of most local competitors who rely on Facebook alone.</p>
        <p>Our <a href="/lp/7-day-website">7-Day Website Package</a>, with <a href="/lp/7-day-website/ph">Philippine pricing from ₱19,999</a>, is built for exactly this: a professional home base for your business, live in a week, that works hand in hand with your Facebook page.</p>
