      slug: 'mobile-first-web-design',
      title: "Mobile-First Web Design: Why Your Website Must Be Built for Phones First",
      category: 'Design',
      date: '18 Jun 2026',
      readTime: '3 min read',
      author: 'Lord Arcamo',
      authorRole: 'Founder',
      authorPhoto: '/images/team-lord.jpeg',
      image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1497091071254-cc9b2ba7c48a?w=1200&h=630&fit=crop&auto=format&q=80',
      excerpt: "Most of your customers will see your website on a phone. Here's what mobile-first design really means, why Google cares, and how to get it right for your business.",
      content: `
        <p>For most small businesses, the majority of website visitors now arrive on a phone, and in the Philippines that share is especially high. Yet many websites are still designed on a large desktop monitor and then squeezed down to fit a phone as an afterthought. The result is cramped layouts, tiny buttons and slow pages. Mobile-first design flips the process, and it is no longer optional.</p>
        <h2>What mobile-first actually means</h2>
        <p>Responsive design means a site adapts to different screen sizes. Mobile-first goes further: you <strong>design and build for the smallest screen first</strong>, deciding what truly matters, then progressively enhance the layout for tablets and desktops. Starting small forces clarity. When you only have a narrow column to work with, you have to decide what your visitor needs most and cut everything else.</p>
        <h2>Google ranks the mobile version of your site</h2>
        <p>Google uses mobile-first indexing, which means it primarily looks at the mobile version of your website when deciding how to rank it. If content, headings or structured data are missing on mobile, or the mobile experience is poor, your rankings suffer on every device, including desktop. A beautiful desktop site with a broken mobile version is, from Google's perspective, a broken site.</p>
        <h2>Design for thumbs, not cursors</h2>
        <p>On a phone, people navigate with their thumb, often one-handed. That changes everything. Buttons should be large enough to tap comfortably, generally at least 44 pixels tall, with space between them. Place your primary call to action where thumbs naturally reach. Make phone numbers tappable to call and addresses tappable to open in maps. Avoid hover effects as the only way to reveal important information, because there is no hover on a touchscreen.</p>
        <h2>Prioritise speed on mobile data</h2>
        <p>Mobile visitors are frequently on slower or patchy connections. Every heavy image, video background and script costs more on mobile than on office Wi-Fi. Mobile-first design means compressing images aggressively, avoiding autoplay videos, loading only what the first screen needs and deferring everything else. A site that loads in two seconds on a phone will outperform a prettier one that takes eight.</p>
        <h2>Keep content readable</h2>
        <p>Use a body text size of at least 16 pixels so visitors never need to zoom. Keep paragraphs short. Use clear headings so people can scan. Ensure strong contrast between text and background, especially for people reading outdoors in bright sunlight. Simplify navigation into a clear menu with only the most important pages.</p>
        <h2>Forms and checkout need extra care</h2>
        <p>Typing on a phone is tedious. Ask only for information you truly need. Use the correct input types so phones show a number pad for phone numbers and an email keyboard for email addresses. Enable autofill. For many local businesses, offering a Messenger or click-to-call option alongside the form captures leads who would otherwise give up.</p>
        <h2>How to test your own site</h2>
        <p>Do not rely on shrinking your desktop browser window. Test on real phones, both a recent model and an older budget Android, using mobile data rather than Wi-Fi. Try to complete your most important task, such as booking, buying or enquiring, using only your thumb. Every point of friction you feel, your customers feel too.</p>
        <p>Every website built through our <a href="/lp/7-day-website">7-Day Website Package</a> is designed mobile-first from the very first sketch, tested on real devices, and optimised to load fast on mobile data.</p>
      `,
